# The two halves of the tenure machinery that the benchmark corpus keeps
# apart, in one program.
#
# `xs` is built INSIDE the bind, so it sits above the beat's mark, is copied
# into the carry on every lap, and from the second lap its elements have lived
# a lap and are promoted into a tenure block. `acc` is made BEFORE the first
# bind, so it sits below the mark, and the copy walk repairs it rather than
# copying it out. What goes into `acc` is an element OF `xs`, so a node below
# the mark comes to hold a pointer into tenure — and `k_survives_x` answers
# yes for such a pointer, which is exactly why `k_repaired_settle` leaves it
# where it is.
#
# runtime.c said nothing measured had reached that. This reaches it. The
# eleven benchmarks split into three that tenure and never repair (widebench,
# indexbench, scanbench) and two that repair and never tenure (encodebench,
# pendbench), and none that do both, which is why four earlier attempts
# missed: the shapes are disjoint in everything the corpus runs.
#
# Reaching it is harmless here, and the reason is the fix kanso#1209 landed: a
# beat whose result is a heap value hands its tenure blocks to the depth
# outside instead of freeing them, so the block outlives the read below. That
# is what `ten_blocks=1` beside `ten_frees=1` records. A change that stops
# handing them up turns this fixture red instead of turning some future
# program into a segfault in `k_copy_size`.
import "std/io"
import "std/list"

fn wide n
  fat = (i -> "element {i} with enough bytes in it to be worth promoting")
  list/to_list (list/map (list/take list/naturals n) fat)

fn gathered n xs acc
  stepped (n - 1) xs (push acc xs[n]!)

fn stepped 0 _ acc
  acc

fn stepped n xs acc
  io/write "" . (_ -> gathered n xs acc)

fn loaded acc
  xs = wide 400
  stepped (length xs) xs acc

fn read_back out
  io/write "kept {length out}, first {out[1]!}, last {out[400]!}\n"

fn begin acc0
  io/write "" . (_ -> loaded acc0) . read_back

pub play = begin []
